PTRACE(2)                      P32W Unix                       PTRACE(2)

NAME
       ptrace - trace a child process

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sys/ptrace.h>

       int ptrace(int req, int pid, unsigned addr, unsigned data);

DESCRIPTION
       ptrace lets a parent process, a debugger such as db(1),
       control a child: stop it, read and write its memory and
       registers, and let it go on.

       The child starts it: it calls ptrace(PT_TRACEME, 0, 0, 0)
       (the other arguments are ignored) and then execs the
       program to be debugged. From then on the traced child stops
       whenever a signal is sent to it, ignored signals included,
       except SIGKILL; and it stops with SIGTRAP after each
       successful exec(2), before the new program's first
       instruction. A BREAK instruction (a breakpoint) raises
       SIGTRAP. The parent learns of each stop through wait(2):
       WIFSTOPPED(status) is true and WSTOPSIG(status) is the
       signal.

       While the child is stopped, the parent may make these
       requests, with pid the child's process ID:

       PT_PEEK (1)
              Store the word at the child's address addr in the
              word at the parent's address data.
       PT_POKE (4)
              Write the word data at the child's address addr.
              Text may be written too: the child gets a private
              copy of the page, so other processes running the
              same program are not affected (this is how
              breakpoints are set).
       PT_GETREGS (3)
              Store the child's registers at data: PT_NREGS (17)
              words, r0 to r14, the stack pointer (index 15) and
              the pc (16). r0 reads as 0.
       PT_SETREGS (6)
              Load the child's registers r1 to r14, sp and pc from
              the 17 words at data (word 0 is ignored).
       PT_CONT (7)
              Let the child run on from its pc. data is the signal
              to deliver to it as it goes (it then acts on it as
              usual: default, ignore or handler), or 0 for none.
              A BREAK leaves the pc at the BREAK itself: to go
              on, put the original instruction back first.
       PT_KILL (8)
              Kill the child with SIGKILL. It need not be stopped.

       Requests 2 and 5 are the same as 1 and 4 (V7's separate
       data-space requests). addr must be word-aligned.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       ESRCH   pid is not a traced child of the caller, has
               exited, or (except PT_KILL) is not stopped.
       EFAULT  addr is misaligned or not mapped in the child, or
               data is a bad address in the parent.
       EINVAL  An unknown request, or a PT_CONT signal of 16 or
               more.

SEE ALSO
       db(1), wait(2), signal(2), exec(2)

BUGS
       Unlike V7, PT_PEEK returns the word through a pointer, so a
       word of -1 cannot be mistaken for an error. There is no
       single-step request (db steps by planting breakpoints), no
       attach to a running process, and no way to read the trap
       cause or faulting address. A traced child cannot leave the
       traced state, and if its parent exits it stays stopped until
       killed.
